### Notifications (FCM)

Push is delivered through Firebase Cloud Messaging (HTTP v1), and every
notification is also written to a per-user inbox in the database. The inbox —
not FCM — is the record of what a user was told: a phone that is off,
uninstalled or unregistered simply misses the push and sees the message on next
open. A deployment with no Firebase credentials still records notifications; it
just cannot wake devices (`pushEnabled: false` in the responses).

| Method | Endpoint                                  | Auth  | Description                                  |
|--------|-------------------------------------------|-------|----------------------------------------------|
| POST   | `/api/v1/notifications/devices`           | Yes   | Register/refresh this device's FCM token     |
| GET    | `/api/v1/notifications/devices`           | Yes   | My registered devices                        |
| DELETE | `/api/v1/notifications/devices`           | Yes   | Unregister a token (call on sign-out)        |
| GET    | `/api/v1/notifications`                   | Yes   | Inbox, newest first, with `unreadCount`      |
| GET    | `/api/v1/notifications/unread-count`      | Yes   | Badge count                                  |
| PATCH  | `/api/v1/notifications/{id}/read`         | Yes   | Mark one read (idempotent)                   |
| PATCH  | `/api/v1/notifications/read-all`          | Yes   | Mark everything read                         |
| DELETE | `/api/v1/notifications/{id}`              | Yes   | Delete one from my inbox                     |
| POST   | `/api/v1/admin/notifications/send`        | Admin | Broadcast to ALL / USER_IDS / FILTER         |
| GET    | `/api/v1/admin/notifications/campaigns`   | Admin | Broadcast history with delivery stats        |
| GET    | `/api/v1/admin/notifications/campaigns/{id}` | Admin | One broadcast, incl. live progress        |

Client contract: register the token after every sign-in and on Firebase's
`onTokenRefresh` (registration is idempotent — re-sending the same token updates
the existing row rather than creating a duplicate), and pass `fcmToken` to
`POST /auth/logout` so the handset stops receiving the signed-out user's
notifications.

To send a notification from server code, call
`notificationService.notifyUser(userId, { title, body, type, data })` — it
writes the inbox row and pushes to every device the user has registered, and
never throws if the push fails.

### App update

The client calls `GET /api/v1/app/version` at launch — unauthenticated, because
a build old enough to need forcing may be too old to hold a valid session — and
switches on `updateAction`:

- `NONE` — up to date, show nothing.
- `OPTIONAL` — offer the update, dismissible.
- `FORCE` — block the app behind an update wall.

`FORCE` is returned when the build is below `minSupportedVersion`, or when the
operator marked the current release `mandatory`. Anything the server cannot
judge (no release published, an unparseable version) resolves to `NONE`, so a
misconfiguration can never lock the user base out.

| Method | Endpoint                                       | Auth  | Description                                   |
|--------|------------------------------------------------|-------|-----------------------------------------------|
| GET    | `/api/v1/app/version?platform=&version=`       | No    | Should this build update?                     |
| GET    | `/api/v1/admin/app-versions`                   | Admin | Published release per platform                |
| PUT    | `/api/v1/admin/app-versions/{platform}`        | Admin | Publish/edit a release (`notify: true` to announce) |
| POST   | `/api/v1/admin/app-versions/{platform}/notify` | Admin | Push "update available" to users on an older build |

The update push targets only users whose registered device on that platform
reports a version older than the published release, so nobody already current is
nagged. Its `data` block carries `latestVersion`, `storeUrl` and `forceUpdate`,
so tapping it can open the store or raise the update wall with no further call.

A ready-to-import Postman collection is included: `postman_collection.json`.

## Environment Variables

See `.env.example` for the full list with descriptions. Required at boot:
`MONGODB_URI`, `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET`, `JWT_REFRESH_SECRET`, `MSG91_AUTH_KEY`,
`MSG91_TEMPLATE_ID`, `MSG91_SENDER_ID`. The server refuses to start if any
are missing.

## Adding Future Modules

The original `FUTURE MODULES` list (roles, admin panel, notifications,
payments, diagnostics, IVS verification, records, support, settings) has since
been largely built out — notifications and the app-update gate are documented
above. The MVC layout, service-based
business logic, and versioned routing (`/api/v1`) mean new modules can be
added as new `models/routes/services/controllers` files without touching
existing ones.
